WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.211
Two quantity statements can concern overlapping categories without describing the same members.

2
00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:09.945
A shared category does not itself guarantee a new overlap.

3
00:00:10.245 --> 00:00:12.059
Some volunteers speak Spanish.

4
00:00:12.359 --> 00:00:14.257
Some Spanish speakers are trained.

5
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:18.333
The first statement guarantees one volunteer who speaks Spanish.

6
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:24.009
The second guarantees a trained Spanish speaker, but does not say that person volunteers.

7
00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:30.645
Put those properties on two different people and both sentences remain true without any trained volunteer.

8
00:00:30.945 --> 00:00:34.081
The proposed overlap is possible, but not required.

9
00:00:34.381 --> 00:00:39.395
This prevents plausible combinations from being mistaken for proven inferences.

10
00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:43.343
A valid-looking sequence of some or most labels is not enough.

11
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.269
Some in one premise may name different people from some in another.

12
00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:50.513
Most in two premises may count different groups.

13
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:52.648
Keep both possibilities visible.

14
00:00:52.948 --> 00:00:55.081
Distinguish possible from guaranteed.

15
00:00:55.381 --> 00:01:01.483
When the overlap fails, say which connection is missing rather than concluding that the groups never overlap.

16
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:06.540
The premises may permit both an overlapping arrangement and a nonoverlapping one.
